      "\"The patch is not closing,\" Yorke says.",
      "The room goes quiet the way a room goes quiet when someone says a thing that everyone was waiting for but nobody wanted to hear first.",
      "\"It is drifting west. It has been drifting west for ten months. I have been measuring from a point that is itself moving. The loss is not an ending. It is a journey.\" Yorke holds up her hand. The number is smudged but legible. \"Eleven point five. This morning. Eleven point six yesterday. The eastern edge is not there anymore. Imo's people did not sail into the dark. The dark sailed to them.\"",
      "Kael Voss puts his cup down. He does not look at Emiko, who is sitting beside him, though he wants to. \"How fast?\"",
      "\"I don't know. I need two more survey posts to calculate velocity. Three to be sure.\"",
      "\"How long do we have?\"",
      "\"I don't know.\"",
      "\"Then what do you know?\"",
      "Yorke looks at him. Kael has spent nine years welding survey posts to the deck of a boat that is itself floating on water he cannot see the bottom of, and she is about to tell him that the deck is moving, and she does not know how to say it gently because there is no gentle way to say that the ground under your boots has always been a tide.",
      "\"I know that we have been following it west without knowing we were following it,\" she says. \"I know that every measurement I have taken has been from a boat that is itself drifting east relative to the patch, which means the patch is moving faster than we are. I know that if we stay where we are, we will be outside it in less than a year. Maybe six months. Maybe three. I don't know.\"",
      "Seo-yun Park speaks from the corner. She has not spoken in twenty minutes because she has been watching the refugees through the porthole, and the refugees are still behind their rope, and the rope is still doing whatever a rope does. \"Three days,\" she says.",
      "Everyone looks at her.",
      "\"For the fever. I will know in three days.\" Seo-yun turns from the porthole. \"But I will tell you now what I suspect. They do not have anything we do not have. They have the same water, the same fish, the same air. The only thing they have that we do not have is the memory of what it was like to be outside the light. And that is not a disease you can quarantine with a rope.\"",
      "Reika picks up her cup and puts it down again without drinking. \"What are you saying?\"",
      "\"I am saying that in three days I will tell you whether they are sick. I am saying that in three days you will have to decide whether to let them in or keep them out, and I am saying that the rope is not what is keeping us safe. The rope is what is keeping us from having to look at them.\"",
