      "They finish the post at dusk. The light changes from gold to red to the colour of old blood, and then it is gone, and they are in the dark, and Kael climbs back onto the deck and Emiko hands him a towel and Yorke takes one last reading with a torch in her mouth, and the reading is worse than the last one, and she writes it down anyway, because that is what engineers do: they write down the numbers even when the numbers are wrong, especially when the numbers are wrong.",
      "They motor back toward the city. The boats are lights in the distance, fifty-eight points of yellow and white in the dark, and Yorke counts them as they approach, the way she has counted them every night for nine years, and she understands that someday soon she will count fifty-seven, or fifty-six, or fewer, and she does not know which ones will be gone, or why, or where they will go, and she does not know if she will be on one of the boats that leaves or one of the boats that stays, and she does not know which is worse.",
